## Run it

```bash
uv sync
uv run predict.py new_data.csv -o predictions.csv
```

Add `--with-input` to keep the input columns alongside the predictions. Input may
be CSV, TSV or Parquet; output format follows the `-o` extension.

## Use it from Python

```python
import pandas as pd
from predict import predict

frame = pd.read_csv("new_data.csv")
predictions = predict(frame)
```

`predict` returns a frame aligned to the input index: a `prediction` column, plus
one `proba_<class>` column per class for classification.

## What is in here

| file | what it is |
| --- | --- |
| `predict.py` | The inference entry point. |
| `preprocess.py` | Imputation and encoding — the exact code used at training time. |
| `preprocessor.json` | Fitted state: imputation values, category vocabularies, column order. |
| `model.json` | The XGBoost model. |
| `inference_metadata.json` | Task type, class labels, required input columns. |
| `pyproject.toml` | Runtime dependencies. |

Nothing here imports `autoxgb_agent` — the bundle stands alone.

## Required input columns

${REQUIRED_COLUMNS}

Extra columns are ignored. Category values not seen during training fall into an
`__other__` bucket rather than failing.

## Before you trust it elsewhere

These metrics come from a held-out split of the **same dataset this model was
trained on**. Gradient-boosted trees do not transfer across data sources with the
same schema but different distributions — another factory, another region,
another time period. Before reusing this model on a new source, score it on
labelled rows from that source; if the primary metric drops materially, retrain
rather than deploying it.
